        <body>
            
            <div type="edition" xml:lang="tam-Latn" rendition="class:tamil maturity:83215">
                                
                <p>
                    <lb n="1"/>putta-ciṅka-nāyakar
                </p>
                
            </div>
            
            <div type="apparatus">
             
            </div>
            
            <div type="translation" source="bib:Ramachandran1954_01">
                
                <p n="1">“The Nāyakar <supplied reason="explanation">i.e., Lord Buddha</supplied> set up by or of Buddha Ciṅka <supplied reason="explanation">Buddha-Siṃha</supplied>” or “The Lord Buddha-Siṃha or Śākyasiṃha”.</p>	
                
            </div>
            
            <div type="translation" resp="part:emfr">
             <p n="1">“The Lord Buddha Siṃha” or “The Lord of Buddha Siṃha”.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div type="commentary">
                <p><bibl><ptr target="bib:Ramachandran1954_01"/></bibl> comments thus: <quote>Śākyasiṃha is the name of the historical
Buddha.</quote></p>
            </div>
            
            <div type="bibliography">
                
                <p>Edited in <bibl><ptr target="bib:Ramachandran1954_01"/></bibl>, with a facsimile (p. 108, no. 130 and pl. XIX, no. 64). See also <bibl><ptr target="bib:Ramachandran1965_01"/></bibl>, <bibl><ptr target="bib:Ramachandran1992_01"/></bibl>, <bibl><ptr target="bib:Ramachandran2005_01"/></bibl> (p. 124, no. 130).</p>
                <p>Edited and translated here by Emmanuel Francis, based on <bibl><ptr target="bib:Ramachandran1954_01"/></bibl> and the facsimile therein.</p>
                
